&lt;h2 id="garden-description">Garden Description&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>This large house (1500m²) laid-out around a peristyle-garden is known for its mosaic pavements figuring Homeric scenes. The peristyle is entered from the S. The N wing had a vast &lt;em>oecus-triclinium&lt;/em> bordered on both sides by two apartments that each had two rooms and a passage to a pseudo-atrium (Plan view, Fig.1).&lt;/p>
&lt;p>The peristyle was trapezoidal in shape (8.29 x 9 x 16. 15 m) and was bordered by 12 columns. Four porticos, 5m wide, enclosed the garden. The semicircular pool facing the &lt;em>oecus-triclinium&lt;/em>, that projected into the garden was paved with a mosaic depicting a mask of Ocean among fishes and had an inscription &amp;quot;NYMFARUM DOMUS&amp;quot; quoting a passage from Virgil's Aeneid. According to the excavator, J.-P. Darmon, a row of broken amphoras that had served as planting pots were found along the central axis of the garden facing the &lt;em>oecus&lt;/em> and the basin (Fig.2)&lt;/p>
